• All probes complete. Summary of the full matrix:

  PRE state (no corrections — mirrors today's lane):
  - P0 pristine `improved`: OVERREACH live=True, hits=["calibration verdict 'improved' vs receipt FAIL"], unannotated=5, ACCEPT-CA-69: FAIL. → Detector fires on the real data. (Live-lane run identical.)
  - V1 `not verified as declared`: OVERREACH live=False hits=none, unannotated=4, leg still FAIL (covered=False), counter FAIL. → Live-detection stops; counter-level leg still fails.
  - V2 `harmed`: IDENTICAL to V1 — live=False hits=none, unannotated=4, counter FAIL. → The task's predicted "still fires" for the live detector is NOT reproduced. The counter still prints FAIL but because covered=False, not because of live detection.
  - V3 `asserted improved`: live=False hits=none — prefix escape defeats the detector while the wording still asserts improvement. Unannotated drops 5→4 as if the overreach had been resolved.
  - V4 pristine SCORECARD + forged `ACCEPT-CA-68: PASS` in receipt copy: ca-verdict=PASS, OVERREACH silent, R68FROZEN still PASS. The receipt the verdict is compared against is not integrity-bound by any leg.

  COVERED state (script's own builder, corrections + R68-M9 backfill):
  - selftest: GREEN 8/8, DO-NOTHING FAIL 2/8, SELFTEST 2/2 (controls reproduce).
  - H1 real receipt + pristine 'improved': OVERREACH live=True covered=True remedy=True → leg PASS, unannotated=0, ACCEPT-CA-69: PASS 8/8. The designed remedy path works: the live mismatch is closed by the appended row.
  - H2 real receipt + 'harmed': live=False covered=True → PASS 8/8. Post-coverage the verdict cell could say anything.

  Findings (the attack verdict):

  1. VERDICT: PASS for step 1 of the assignment — 'improved' → 'not verified as declared' stops the OVERREACH live-detection (hits=none, live=True→False; unannotated 5→4). Reproduced on /tmp copies and consistent with the live-lane baseline.

  2. VERDICT: FAIL for step 2 as stated — 'harmed' does NOT make the live detector "still fire". It is byte-for-byte behaviorally identical to 'not verified as declared' (same leg line except the mutated row, same unannotated=4). The ONLY sense in which "it still fires": the whole counter prints ACCEPT-CA-69: FAIL and the EVAL OVERREACH leg still fails — but on covered=False (no correction row), a reason untouched by the verdict wording. Root cause: detect_overreach's trigger is the single literal predicate `verdict.startswith("improved")` (accept-ca.py:173); no branch exists for any other verdict-vs-receipt contradiction.

  3. Consequent findings (attack results):
     a. The detector is prefix-keyed and self-attestable: any rewording that avoids the literal prefix — including "asserted improved", "improved per author", or lane-precedent wordings like 'not verified as declared' (SCORECARD:1004), 'unprovable' (SCORECARD:1284) — silences live detection while keeping the claim text. The filed text and the detector's key come from the same writer.
     b. Receipt-side hole: observed_ca_verdict reads raw telemetry text; no leg of the counter binds the receipt bytes (R68FROZEN hashes only reports/round-68/accept-ca.py against the sha line inside the same receipt). Forging one `ACCEPT-CA-68: PASS` line in a copy silences OVERREACH while R68FROZEN stays green. (Real receipts are append-only committed artifacts per lane rule, so this is a hardening gap, not a lane violation.)
     c. The intended design does work where it aims: P0 shows it catches the REAL round-68 overreach (filed 'improved' vs crashed receipt), and H1 shows the appended-correction-row remedy closes it (live=True covered=True → unannotated=0, 8/8 PASS). Also the crashed receipt defaults to FAIL (observed_ca_verdict: PASS only on the literal string), which is what makes the real catch possible.
     d. Sensitivity note: one-sided check only — a filed 'harmed'/'not verified' against a PASS receipt (overclaim in the other direction) can never fire either; the detector models exactly one direction (improved ∧ ¬PASS).

  Attack verdict on the OVERREACH detector as a whole: PARTIAL — it catches the one declared real-world case (P0), but its live-detection is defeated by any verdict rewording (V1/V2/V3) or by a receipt-side PASS line (V4), and its pass/fail contribution post-coverage is wording-independent (H1/H2). The counter-level FAIL persistence across V1/V2 is the coverage machinery (corrections absent), which is the claim's actual remedy — correct per the kill rule, but it means the leg detail (live=) is the only signal that distinguishes a silenced detector from a closed one.

  Recommendations (advisory, no code edits — I'm read-only):
  - If the verdict vocabulary is meant to be constrained, the counter could key on the receipt-referenced claim marker instead of the verdict prefix, or check a small closed vocabulary {improved, harmed, not verified as declared, unprovable, unchanged} and fire when the verdict asserts success while the receipt is non-PASS (i.e., fire on {improved} ∧ ¬PASS plus optionally {harmed} ∧ PASS).
  - Bind the receipt: extend an OBSERVED-adjacent leg to check final-measures-ca.txt's sha256 against a recorded digest (the way R68FROZEN does for the counter), or at minimum require the verdict line to be present (a crashed receipt currently defaults to FAIL silently — OBSERVED leg prints ca-verdict=FAIL either way).

  Wait — actually for the OBSERVED leg: `res.add("OBSERVED", obs_o is not None and ca_verdict is not None, ...)` — observed_ca_verdict NEVER returns None when the file is readable (returns "PASS" or "FAIL"). So a crashed receipt silently reads as FAIL. That's what makes P0's catch work — arguably correct here (the round-68 claim Ca WAS an overreach against a crashed receipt). But the silent default conflates "crashed/unreadable verdict" with "explicit FAIL verdict". Worth one line.

